  completeExplanation: 'Methods of Separation in Everyday Life explores how substances in mixtures are isolated based on distinct physical properties. The chapter opens on page 163 with Kabir\'s famous doha: "साधु ऐसा चाहिए जैसा सूप सुभाय । सार सार को गहि रहै थोथा दई उड़ाय ॥" comparing the discerning mind of a wise person to a winnowing tray (soop) that retains nutritious grain while letting useless chaff fly away. The concepts unfold through the travels of Malli and Valli across India:\n\n1. Solid-Solid Mixtures: In Haryana, they observe Handpicking, where small quantities of visibly different impurities (stones, husk) are removed by hand from wheat/rice based on colour, shape, or size. They watch Threshing, where harvested wheat bundles are struck on wooden logs to dislodge edible grains. Winnowing uses the kinetic action of moving air or wind to blow away light husk while dense grain falls vertically into a heap below (demonstrated via rubbing and blowing peanut skins). In Gujarat, Sieving is used to separate bran from wheat flour for meethi puri; fine flour passes through the mesh while coarser bran remains on top. At construction sites, large inclined wire sieves separate coarse stones and pebbles from fine construction sand.\n\n2. Soluble Solids in Liquids: At Ahmedabad\'s Sabarmati Ashram, the historical Namak Satyagraha (1930) connects science to civic history. Common salt is harvested by trapping seawater in shallow coastal beds (salt pans) where solar heat and wind cause Evaporation of water, leaving behind crude crystalline salt, also harvested at inland salt lakes like Rajasthan\'s Sambhar Lake. Condensation cools water vapour back into liquid droplets (demonstrated with a kettle and cold plate), allowing pure water recovery. In Ayurveda, medicinal plant parts are dried in shade to evaporate water without destroying volatile chemical actives.\n\n3. Insoluble Solids in Liquids: In Puducherry, making tea demonstrates Sedimentation (heavier insoluble tea leaves settling at the bottom) followed by Decantation (gently pouring out liquid without disturbing the sediment). Decantation is also used to wash rice/pulses and to skim off oil from an immiscible oil-water mixture. Because decantation cannot remove fine suspended particles, Filtration is employed using a porous barrier. In the laboratory, circular filter paper is folded twice into a cone and placed in a funnel; solid mud caught on paper is the residue, while clear water passing through is the filtrate. Other filter media include muslin cloth, sand, and charcoal. Biological filtration is seen in nasal hairs trapping dust and N95 masks intercepting airborne microbes. Tea bags evolved from natural silk to gauze and modern porous paper.\n\n4. Liquid-Liquid / Density Separation & Magnetic Separation: In Bhopal, Churning (manthan) of curd using a wooden churner (mathni) separates low-density butter fat globules (which float) from denser liquid buttermilk (chhach). In Shillong, a magnet is used to attract iron nails from non-magnetic sawdust (Magnetic Separation), widely applied in waste management and scrap recycling cranes.\n\n5. Multi-Step Synthesis & Environmental Ethics: Complex mixtures require sequential methods (e.g. separating iron nails, stones, black pepper, sand, salt, and water). Finally, the poem "Koilas" highlights plastic pollution choking river aquatic life, urging pollution abatement at source.',
